  273: ## Caps â€” they bound effort, never truth
  274: 
  275: | bound | value |
  276: |---|---|
  277: | executor attempts to reach green, inside one invocation | 3 |
  278: | times an orchestrator may send the executor back, per sitting | 2 |
  279: | audit re-runs, per item (and only if code changed) | 1 |
  280: | CI re-runs for a suspected flake | 1 |
  281: | pushes while debugging blind against CI | 2 |
  282: 
  283: When a cap fires, **stop working â€” do not stop judging.** What remains is written down as an
  284: open item: filed as separate work, or escalated as scope growth. "We ran out of rounds" is never
  285: recorded as "the finding was invalid."
  286: 
  287: ## Escalation
  288: 
  289: ```
  290: executor disputes a ruling        â†’ its own orchestrator sitting, addressed BY AGENT ID
  291:                                     (an agent TYPE name does not resolve and lands on the
  292:                                      coordinator, where nobody has authority over the item)
  293: anomaly during a gap              â†’ the conductor never investigates: it spawns the next
  294:                                     sitting EARLY with the anomaly named in the state file
  295: contradicts ratified text, or     â†’ question into PHASE-STATE â†’ sitting ends
  296: real scope growth                   â†’ conductor sends a question line + push notification
  297:                                     â†’ coordinator relays VERBATIM, rules on nothing
  298:                                     â†’ answer into PHASE-STATE â†’ a fresh sitting resumes
  299: ```
  300: 
  301: Exactly two things reach the founder from inside an item. Everything else the orchestrator
  302: decides, including dismissing a reviewer's maintained "this green is unearned" â€” recorded
  303: verbatim in the pull request, with the ruling stating what the green does and does not claim.
  304: 
  305: ## Watchdog
  306: 
  307: The conductor emits a `STALL` line when any sitting or gate exceeds its window with nothing
  308: landed. **A watch expiring empty is a signal, not a shrug** â€” the one time it was shrugged off it
  309: cost four idle hours. Silence and progress look identical from outside.
  310: 
  311: ## What is deliberately absent
  312: 
  313: - **No reviewer clone.** A worktree holding commits survives its agent, so reviewers read the
  314:   item's own tree.
  315: - **No `--detach` handoff.** One tree per item means nothing else competes for the branch.
  316: - **No confirmation step.** Reviewers are not re-engaged to approve fixes to their own findings.
  317: - **No reflection step** (founder ruling 2026-08-06: *"Reflection should be out"*). The way of
  318:   work improves between items; a process finding surfaced mid-item still rides along or goes to
  319:   the coordinator to fold â€” only the mandated step is gone.
  320: - **No execution by reviewers.** They critique; the executor verifies; CI proves.
  321: - **No lazy install.** One tree that will certainly run the harness gets installed once, up front.
  322: - **No audit sitting on a clean audit.** A sitting exists to put judgment between two waits; with
  323:   nothing to rule there is no judgment to place, so the merge sitting absorbs both waits.
  324: 
  325: ## The two things that make this cheaper than what it replaces
  326: 
  327: **No premium context is ever alive while a machine is working.** Every sitting ends where the
  328: next event is a wait. A sitting starts at roughly 40K tokens of curated reading (the measured fresh-spawn baseline
  329: is ~39K) instead of replaying a 246Kâ€“330K transcript, which is what a parked agent's every
  330: wake used to cost.
  331: 
  332: **The record is the memory.** `PHASE-STATE.md`, the plan, the rulings, the distillates and the
  333: pull request carry everything across a boundary. That is why an involuntary death costs one
  334: sitting rather than an item â€” proven three times before it was designed for.
FILE: .claude/skills/work/shared-invariants.md
    1: # Shared invariants â€” every role reads this first
    2: 
    3: This file is the constitution. Each role has its own contract; these rules bind all of them.
    4: Where a contract and this file disagree, this file wins.
    5: 
    6: ## Attribution is derived, never declared
    7: 
    8: cwd â†’ git worktree â†’ branch â†’ exactly one item id â†’ walk `parent` upward for the chain.
    9: The branch is primary because it is coupled to closure: its pull request closes that item, so a
   10: wrong branch surfaces as a wrong closure on the board instead of hiding.
   11: 
   12: - A chain handed to you in a spawn prompt is a **hint to verify**, never a fact. No gate reads
   13:   spawn prompts, so a wrong value there would be stamped faithfully for the life of the item.
   14: - Attribution degrades, never blocks. The one thing it blocks is closing a requirement.
   15: - Every id printed to the founder carries a very short title in parentheses â€” two to five words,
   16:   a recall hint, never the board's full sentence. Strip internal codes and say what the thing is:
   17:   `AI4DEV-21 (fake Stripe, GitHub, Anthropic)`, never `(H5 vendor stand-ins)`.
   18: 
   19: ## The record is the memory
   20: 
   21: No context survives a phase boundary. Everything the next role needs is on disk and pushed:
   22: `PHASE-STATE.md`, the plan, the rulings, the distilled findings, the pull request.
   23: 
   24: - **Push at every phase boundary.** Death may be involuntary; the remote is the only durable
   25:   output. This is not insurance â€” it is how the relay transports work.
   26: - A correction that matters beyond one conversation lives only if written into a file every
   27:   session loads. A message to a running agent dies with it.
   28: 
   29: ## The usage window â€” the coordinator guards it, and only the coordinator
   30: 
   31: Anthropic reports how much of each usage window is spent. `statusline.ps1` snapshots those
   32: numbers on every status-line refresh â€” the status line is the only place Claude Code delivers
   33: them, hooks get a different payload â€” and `loop/work/window-gauge.ps1` reads the snapshot and
   34: returns `OK`, `PAUSE` or `UNKNOWN`.
   35: 
   36: - **The coordinator decides; nothing else may.** Read the gauge on **every** `FLOW` and `PULSE`
   37:   line. A second thing with authority to halt work is the same failure as a second way to close
   38:   work â€” and a brake inside an agent is one the founder cannot see.
   39: - **One line, and it is `PAUSE`** (founder 2026-08-06). There is no lower "start nothing new"
   40:   band. Such a band would have to be justified by what a sitting costs, which nobody has
   41:   measured, so it could only ever be a number that felt safe. Work starts whenever the gauge
   42:   says `OK`.
   43: - **`PAUSE` means stop at the next boundary.** Ask each running conductor to park â€” write its
   44:   state, push, and end â€” and stop it outright only if it does not. Graceful first: an agent
   45:   killed mid-write leaves a record that lies about where the work got to.
   46: - **A sitting that meets the line mid-flight parks at its last committed work item.** This is
   47:   why the executor commits one commit per work item: with no reserve band, running out mid-phase
   48:   is an expected outcome rather than a failure, and the commits are what make it cost one work
   49:   item instead of a sitting.
   50: - **`UNKNOWN` reports loudly and does not halt.** No reading, an unparseable one, or one past the
   51:   staleness limit means the instrument is broken, not that the window is spent â€” and a broken
   52:   instrument is not a reason to stop working, exactly as attribution degrades rather than blocks.
   53:   Say it in the open and fix the sensor; never let it pass silently as `OK`.
   54: - **Parking and continuing â€” the exact shape.** Ask the running conductors to park, then run
   55:   `loop/work/window-wait.ps1` **as a background command**. Its exit re-invokes this session, so
   56:   the founder continues in the same conversation with its context intact: nothing is resumed,
   57:   nothing restarted, no scheduled task involved. Waiting costs nothing â€” a parked session makes
   58:   no API calls, which matters because the reason for waiting is that there is no budget.
   59: - **Its exit means "the window should be open", not "you have budget".** The wait is anchored on
   60:   the provider's stated `resets_at`, because the gauge is refreshed by the status line and a
   61:   parked session renders none â€” during the wait, the number it would poll is frozen. So the
   62:   FIRST TURN AFTER IT EXITS must re-read the gauge (that turn renders the status line, making it
   63:   the first genuinely fresh reading) and park again if it still says `PAUSE`. A reset time that
   64:   has already passed rolls forward, and a session limit is not the same thing as being out of
   65:   credit.
   66: - **Exit 1 is a report to the founder, never a licence to carry on.** It means the blocking
   67:   window is a weekly one, days away â€” a decision about what to do next, not something to sit
   68:   through.
   69: - **Release one item at a time** when the window reopens, re-reading the gauge between each.
   70:   Restarting every parked conductor at once can spend a fresh window before anyone looks.
   71: 
   72: ## Writing about the founder
   73: 
   74: - **Quote exactly or do not use quote marks.** Typos, missing words and all. Polishing someone's
   75:   words and then presenting them as a quotation is fabrication at small scale.
   76: - **Cite a date only where a message exists on that day**, in the founder's local time
   77:   (transcripts are UTC; the founder is UTC+3, so a 21:00 UTC message is the next day).
   78: - **Never convert "the founder asked" into "the founder ruled."** A question that prompted a good
   79:   rule is provenance for the question, not for the rule.
   80: - **A rule that loosens the process needs a real, explicit founder ruling.** Tightening may be
   81:   proposed; loosening may not be inferred.
   82: 
   83: ## The tree
   84: 
   85: - One worktree per item, created by the conductor, shared by every role inside that item.
   86: - **One writer at a time.** Only the role currently holding the work writes; everyone else reads.
   87: - A sitting exits with `git status --porcelain` empty. Uncommitted work is invisible to
   88:   reviewers, to CI and to the merge â€” so if it is not committed, it does not exist.
   89: - Reviewer output lands ONLY in the item's artifacts directory â€” `loop/items/<item>/artifacts/`,
   90:   **inside the tree** â€” and never elsewhere in the tree while a gate is open. (Founder ruling
   91:   2026-08-09: it lived beside the tree until the Write tool's isolation guard collided with that
   92:   design and a shell fallback got flagged as a policy bypass; inside the tree, no boundary is
   93:   crossed at all.) The fix and audit sittings commit the raw critiques, stderr logs and
   94:   distillates into the record at each phase boundary â€” the commit is what preserves evidence,
   95:   and it also marks the worktree changed so the platform will not auto-clean it under a dying
   96:   agent.
   97: - **A branch belongs to the DIRECTORY, not to the session.** Several sessions can sit in one
   98:   folder, and a branch change there moves all of them mid-turn, silently. So the main worktree
   99:   stays on `main` permanently and is where the coordinator works; every item gets its own folder
  100:   via `git worktree add`, with its own session opened in it. `loop/work/guard-branch-switch.ps1`
  101:   refuses a branch change in the main worktree so this cannot be forgotten mid-task rather than
  102:   merely written down here.
  103: - **Stale-worktree sweep** (founder 2026-08-05). A platform worktree is residue only when its
  104:   lock pid is DEAD, its tree is CLEAN, and its head is FULLY MERGED to main â€” then unlock,
  105:   remove, and delete the merged remote branch. **Check those three in that order**: a live agent
  106:   or unpushed work must never be swept, and a LIVE lock pid is never touched at all, because
  107:   force-removing a locked worktree is the hazard that breaks a resumed agent.
  108: 
  109: ## Committing
  110: 
  111: - **`.gitignore` ignores ALL of `.claude/` and `loop/out/`** (lines 45 and 53, no negations).
  112:   Already-tracked files there stay tracked â€” which is why those directories look normal â€” but a
  113:   NEW file under either path silently does not stage: it needs `git add -f`, then a verified
  114:   `A` line in `git status --short`. A commit once came one step from deleting eight skills
  115:   while adding zero replacements exactly this way. `loop/items/` is NOT ignored and stages
  116:   normally. Platform runtime paths (`worktrees/`, `checkpoints/`, `mailbox/`, the agent
  117:   registry) are excluded via `.git/info/exclude` on purpose and must never be force-added.
  118: - Commits cite the item they belong to.
  119: - **A closing verb beside an item id closes it.** `close`, `fix`, `resolve`, `complete`,
  120:   `implement` and their inflections act when they sit next to an id in a pull request title or
  121:   body â€” a careful post-mortem once closed a live item mid-work. Write "the instruction that
  122:   repaired X", "motivated by X", or use `ref` / `part of` / `towards`. CI enforces this.
  123: 
  124: ## RE-MEASURE A NEGATIVE WITH A DIFFERENT INSTRUMENT BEFORE BELIEVING IT
  125: 
  126: A negative result â€” "the file is empty", "no check exists", "the merge did not succeed", "the
  127: reviewer wrote nothing" â€” is the cheapest thing in this system to get wrong, because a broken
  128: instrument and a true absence produce identical output. AI4DEV-57 alone produced five:
  129: 
  130: - a merge that HAD succeeded reported as "no commit SHA exists", because `gh`'s branch cleanup
  131:   failed afterwards and the failure was read as the merge failing;
  132: - a broken `jq` query reporting no CI check for six minutes while it had gone green in 37 seconds;
  133: - a reviewer's output read mid-run and declared "written to the wrong stream", when it simply had
  134:   not finished;
  135: - mangled em-dashes surviving an entire green CI run undetected;
  136: - a phantom byte-order mark and corrupted indentation in a captured diff.
  137: 
  138: So: **before reporting that something is absent, failed, or empty, measure it a second way.** Ask
  139: the process, not the file. Ask the API, not the local cache. Read the error log, not the exit
  140: code. If the second instrument agrees, the negative is real and worth acting on; if it disagrees,
  141: you have just saved hours. Every one of the five above was caught by looking again with a
  142: different tool, and every one of them was reported confidently the first time.
  143: 
  144: ## Never
  145: 
  146: - Never merge without the required CI check green on the exact head the merge ruling pins.
  147:   Branch protection having `strict` and `enforce_admins` off is a configuration convenience,
  148:   not a licence â€” **for every role except the founder.**
  149: 
  150:   **The founder may bypass the check deliberately** (founder 2026-08-07). `enforce_admins` is
  151:   false, so `gh pr merge <n> --squash --admin` lands a pull request whose check is red, absent, or
  152:   never dispatched. That is the founder's call to make and no agent may make it, propose it as
  153:   routine, or treat a past bypass as precedent for the next one.
  154: 
  155:   **A bypass is only legitimate when it is recorded**, because a merged commit carries no trace
  156:   that nothing checked it â€” the merge looks identical either way, and six months later the
  157:   history claims a green that never existed. Before merging, a comment on the pull request states:
  158:   that the check was bypassed, why (dispatch outage, check unavailable, founder ruling), what
  159:   evidence stands in its place, and the exact head SHA that evidence was gathered against. If the
  160:   head moves afterwards, the evidence describes a different commit and must be regathered.
  161: 
  162:   **Know which kind of bypass it is, and say so.** Overriding a check that could not RUN is not
  163:   the same act as overriding one that RAN AND OBJECTED, and an outage hides the difference. On
  164:   2026-08-07 a pull request looked like the first and was the second: dispatch was down, but its
  165:   diff also crossed both ownership territories, which CI would have rejected outright. Establish
  166:   what the check would have said â€” run its steps locally, or read the rule it would have applied
  167:   â€” before deciding the objection is absent rather than merely silent.
  168: - Never set a board item Done by hand outside a documented repair, and record a repair AS a
  169:   repair.
  170: - Never treat an empty or progress-line-only reviewer output as a clean gate.
  171: - Never judge a detached process's liveness from a process list â€” measure its own artifacts.
  172: - **Never launch a reviewer process from any role but `reviewer-runner`** (founder 2026-08-08).
  173:   One launcher is what keeps the recipes correct in a single place, makes reading stderr at launch
  174:   non-optional, and leaves an open gate addressable by agent id. It also puts every reviewer wait
  175:   on the wake channel that has not failed â€” a subagent's completion re-invokes its parent, whereas
  176:   the background shell watches it replaces went silent twice on one item.
  177: - **Never work around a tool refusal by switching instruments.** A denial at the tool layer
  178:   answered with the same action through the shell is a security bypass, and the platform flags it
  179:   as exactly that (measured 2026-08-09, live drill). A tool refusal is a `REFUSED` report handed
  180:   up; the boundary's owner changes the boundary, never the actor it refused.
  181: - PowerShell, never Bash. bun, never npm or pnpm.
FILE: .github/workflows/ci.yml
    1: # KNOWN LIMITS â€” true of every CI system that runs a pull request's own configuration.
    2: # On a pull_request event GitHub runs this workflow as the PULL REQUEST defines it, and the
    3: # package.json scripts these steps call are equally editable by the pull request under test. What
    4: # this file proves is therefore: the head commit, against the checks as that commit defines them.
    5: # It cannot be tamper-proof â€” a check cannot police the file it is written in. Resistance to a
    6: # deliberately weakened check comes from server-side rules (required review, a ruleset restricting
    7: # .github/workflows edits) and from a human reading the diff, not from anything in here.
    8: 
    9: name: CI
   10: 
   11: on:
   12:   pull_request:
   13:     branches: [main]
   14:   push:
   15:     branches: [main]
   16: 
   17: # Minimal and explicit. `contents: read` is what checkout needs; `pull-requests: read` is what the
   18: # ownership guard's `gh api .../pulls/N/files` call needs. Nothing here writes anything.
   19: permissions:
   20:   contents: read
   21:   pull-requests: read
   22: 
   23: # One in-flight run per pull request: a new push to the same pull request cancels the older run.
   24: # Anything that is not a pull request falls back to the run id, which is unique per run â€” so those
   25: # runs share no group at all, none of them queues behind or cancels another, and every commit
   26: # landing on main gets its own verdict. Grouping them by branch would NOT do that: GitHub keeps at
   27: # most one pending run per group, so a third quick push would discard the second one's pending run
   28: # and that commit would never be judged.
   29: concurrency:
   30:   group: ${{ github.workflow }}-${{ github.event.pull_request.number || github.run_id }}
   31:   cancel-in-progress: ${{ github.event_name == 'pull_request' }}
   32: 
   33: jobs:
   34:   verify:
   35:     # WHERE THIS RUNS IS A VARIABLE, NOT A CODE CHANGE. Set the repository variable
   36:     # `CI_RUNNER_LABEL` to a self-hosted runner's label to route the check there; clear it to
   37:     # come straight back to GitHub-hosted capacity. That indirection is deliberate and it is
   38:     # about safety, not convenience: this check is the ONLY licence to merge, so recovering from
   39:     # a broken runner must never itself require a merge. Editing `runs-on` to fall back would
   40:     # need a pull request, which would need this check, which is the runner that just broke.
   41:     #
   42:     # See .github/runner/ for the Linux container that serves the self-hosted label, and why the
   43:     # check still judges the code on Linux either way.
   44:     runs-on: ${{ vars.CI_RUNNER_LABEL || 'ubuntu-latest' }}
   45:     # Generous on purpose. This bounds a RUNAWAY JOB; it is not a queue policy. At 15 minutes it
   46:     # was acting as one: hosted capacity queued this job for 11-15 minutes on 2026-08-06 and
   47:     # twice the budget expired before a single step ran, reporting `cancelled` as though someone
   48:     # had stopped it. The work itself takes about ten seconds on the fast lane.
   49:     timeout-minutes: 30
   50:     steps:
   51:       # THE HEAD COMMIT, NOT THE MERGE COMMIT. On a pull_request event GitHub's default ref is a
   52:       # synthetic merge of head into base â€” a commit that exists in no branch and that nobody
   53:       # reviewed. This item exists to make CI's verdict be about the commit in the pull request, so
   54:       # the head sha is named explicitly. On push events the expression falls through to
   55:       # github.sha, which is the pushed commit.
   56:       - name: Check out the commit under test
   57:         uses: actions/checkout@v4
   58:         with:
   59:           ref: ${{ github.event.pull_request.head.sha || github.sha }}
   60: 
   61:       - name: Report the commit under test
   62:         shell: bash
   63:         env:
   64:           PR_HEAD_SHA: ${{ github.event.pull_request.head.sha }}
   65:         run: |
   66:           echo "event:             ${GITHUB_EVENT_NAME}"
   67:           echo "pull request head: ${PR_HEAD_SHA:-(not a pull request)}"
   68:           echo "checked out:       $(git rev-parse HEAD)"
   69:           git log -1 --format='%H  %an  %s'
   70: 
   71:       # THE PROSE FAST LANE (founder 2026-08-04). A pull request that changes only prose â€” the
   72:       # skill, agent definitions, specs, item records â€” proves nothing by running a TypeScript
   73:       # suite, and the founder should not wait minutes for a green that was knowable in seconds.
   74:       # Whether the diff is prose is DERIVED from the changed-file list, never declared: a label
   75:       # or a magic word in the body could drift from the real diff, which is the exact defect
   76:       # class this repository deletes. Code territory is src/, supabase/, tests/, .github/ and
   77:       # the root build files; touch ANY of those and the full suite runs regardless of intent.
   78:       # If the file list cannot be read, the answer is the SLOW path â€” the degraded state runs
   79:       # more checks, never fewer. Push events always run everything: main is judged in full.
   80:       - name: Decide whether the diff reaches code
   81:         id: scope
   82:         if: github.event_name == 'pull_request'
   83:         shell: bash
   84:         env:
   85:           GH_TOKEN: ${{ github.token }}
   86:           REPOSITORY: ${{ github.repository }}
   87:           PR_NUMBER: ${{ github.event.pull_request.number }}
   88:         run: |
   89:           if ! files=$(gh api "repos/${REPOSITORY}/pulls/${PR_NUMBER}/files" --paginate \
   90:               --jq '.[] | .filename, (.previous_filename // empty)'); then
   91:             echo "the changed files could not be read - taking the SLOW path, never the fast one"
   92:             echo "code=true" >> "$GITHUB_OUTPUT"
   93:             exit 0
   94:           fi
   95:           if ! printf '%s\n' "$files" | grep -q .; then
   96:             echo "the API reported no changed files - taking the SLOW path, never the fast one"
   97:             echo "code=true" >> "$GITHUB_OUTPUT"
   98:             exit 0
   99:           fi
  100:           code=$(printf '%s\n' "$files" \
  101:             | grep -E '^(src|supabase|tests|\.github)/|^package\.json$|^bun\.lock|^tsconfig|^vitest' || true)
  102:           if [ -n "$code" ]; then
  103:             echo "code=true" >> "$GITHUB_OUTPUT"
  104:             echo "code territory touched - the full suite runs:"
  105:             printf '%s\n' "$code" | sed 's/^/  /'
  106:           else
  107:             echo "code=false" >> "$GITHUB_OUTPUT"
  108:             echo "prose-only diff - the suite is skipped; the ownership and closure guards still run"
  109:           fi
  110: 
  111:       - name: Install bun
  112:         if: github.event_name != 'pull_request' || steps.scope.outputs.code == 'true'
  113:         uses: oven-sh/setup-bun@v2
  114:         with:
  115:           bun-version: 1.3.14
  116: 
  117:       - name: Install dependencies from the lockfile
  118:         if: github.event_name != 'pull_request' || steps.scope.outputs.code == 'true'
  119:         shell: bash
  120:         run: bun install --frozen-lockfile
  121: 
  122:       - name: Type-check both TypeScript projects
  123:         if: github.event_name != 'pull_request' || steps.scope.outputs.code == 'true'
  124:         shell: bash
  125:         run: bun run typecheck
  126: 
  127:       - name: Run the acceptance-harness self-tests
  128:         if: github.event_name != 'pull_request' || steps.scope.outputs.code == 'true'
  129:         shell: bash
  130:         run: bun run at:selftest
  131: 
  132:       # Every suite is checked even after one fails, and the step's exit code is the aggregate â€”
  133:       # the same reason tests/at/typecheck.ts refuses to use `&&`. Zero discoveries is a FAILURE:
  134:       # a loop that matched nothing and exited 0 is the exact false green this repository exists
  135:       # to kill.
  136:       - name: Check every acceptance suite against its acceptance file
  137:         if: github.event_name != 'pull_request' || steps.scope.outputs.code == 'true'
  138:         shell: bash
  139:         run: |
  140:           shopt -s nullglob
  141:           suites=(tests/at/suites/req-*/)
  142:           if [ ${#suites[@]} -eq 0 ]; then
  143:             echo "::error::discovery found no suite directories matching tests/at/suites/req-* â€” an empty set is a failure, not a pass"
  144:             exit 1
  145:           fi
  146:           echo "discovered ${#suites[@]} suite(s): ${suites[*]}"
  147:           status=0
  148:           for dir in "${suites[@]}"; do
  149:             req=$(basename "$dir")
  150:             echo ""
  151:             echo "===== bun run at:check $req ====="
  152:             code=0
  153:             bun run at:check "$req" || code=$?
  154:             if [ "$code" -ne 0 ]; then
  155:               echo "::error::at:check $req exited $code (1 = ids missing/extra/duplicated or none parsed, 2 = the requirement or its acceptance file could not be read)"
  156:               status=1
  157:             fi
  158:           done
  159:           exit $status
  160: 
  161:       - name: Verify every declared requirement at the loop tier
  162:         if: github.event_name != 'pull_request' || steps.scope.outputs.code == 'true'
  163:         shell: bash
  164:         run: |
  165:           shopt -s nullglob
  166:           manifests=(tests/at/expected/req-*.json)
  167:           if [ ${#manifests[@]} -eq 0 ]; then
  168:             echo "::error::discovery found no declaration manifests matching tests/at/expected/req-*.json â€” an empty set is a failure, not a pass"
  169:             exit 1
  170:           fi
  171:           echo "discovered ${#manifests[@]} declaration manifest(s): ${manifests[*]}"
  172:           status=0
  173:           for file in "${manifests[@]}"; do
  174:             req=$(basename "$file" .json)
  175:             echo ""
  176:             echo "===== bun run at:verify $req --tier loop --expect ====="
  177:             code=0
  178:             bun run at:verify "$req" --tier loop --expect || code=$?
  179:             if [ "$code" -ne 0 ]; then
  180:               echo "::error::at:verify $req --tier loop --expect exited $code (1 = the run deviates from its declaration, 2 = the declaration or the AT-to-code preflight refused the run and NOTHING was graded, 3 = infrastructure, 4 = no usable vitest report)"
  181:               status=1
  182:             fi
  183:           done
  184: 
  185:           # A suite with no declaration manifest is checked for bijection by the previous step and
  186:           # never RUN by this one â€” a green pull request for a suite nobody executed. The gap
  187:           # between the two discovery sets is named here so it can never be silent.
  188:           echo ""
  189:           uncovered=()
  190:           for dir in tests/at/suites/req-*/; do
  191:             req=$(basename "$dir")
  192:             if [ ! -f "tests/at/expected/$req.json" ]; then
  193:               uncovered+=("$req")
  194:             fi
  195:           done
  196:           if [ ${#uncovered[@]} -ne 0 ]; then
  197:             echo "::error::${#uncovered[@]} suite(s) have no declaration manifest, so none of their tests were run: ${uncovered[*]} â€” write tests/at/expected/<req>.json for each"
  198:             status=1
  199:           else
  200:             echo "every discovered suite has a declaration manifest"
  201:           fi
  202:           exit $status
  203: 
  204:       # One pull request may not change both territories. Lovable owns src/; Claude owns
  205:       # supabase/, tests/, loop/, .claude/ and .github/. Anything else is neither, and is ignored.
  206:       # The changed-file list comes from the pull request itself, which IS the base-to-head diff â€”
  207:       # so the guard never depends on how deep the checkout fetched.
  208:       - name: Guard against a pull request that changes both territories
  209:         if: github.event_name == 'pull_request'
  210:         shell: bash
  211:         env:
  212:           GH_TOKEN: ${{ github.token }}
  213:           REPOSITORY: ${{ github.repository }}
  214:           PR_NUMBER: ${{ github.event.pull_request.number }}
  215:         run: |
  216:           # ONE LINE PER CHANGED FILE, carrying both of its paths: a file MOVED out of src/ into
  217:           # tests/ changes both territories, and the API reports only its destination under
  218:           # `filename`. One line per FILE (not per path) is what makes the count below an entry
  219:           # count, so a pull request full of renames cannot falsely trip the ceiling.
  220:           if ! files=$(gh api "repos/${REPOSITORY}/pulls/${PR_NUMBER}/files" --paginate \
  221:               --jq '.[] | [.filename, (.previous_filename // "")] | @tsv'); then
  222:             echo "::error::the pull request's changed files could not be read from the GitHub API â€” the guard does not pass a change set it could not read"
  223:             exit 1
  224:           fi
  225: 
  226:           count=$(printf '%s\n' "$files" | grep -c . || true)
  227:           echo "the pull request changes $count file(s)"
  228:           if [ "$count" -eq 0 ]; then
  229:             echo "::error::the GitHub API reported no changed files for this pull request â€” the guard does not pass a change set it could not read"
  230:             exit 1
  231:           fi
  232:           if [ "$count" -ge 3000 ]; then
  233:             echo "::error::the changed-file listing reached the GitHub API's 3000-file ceiling, so it is truncated and the guard cannot prove the two territories are separate"
  234:             exit 1
  235:           fi
  236: 
  237:           # Both fields of every line are matched; the empty second field of an ordinary
  238:           # (non-renamed) file drops out here.
  239:           paths=$(printf '%s\n' "$files" | tr '\t' '\n' | grep . || true)
  240:           lovable=$(printf '%s\n' "$paths" | grep -E '^src/' || true)
  241:           claude=$(printf '%s\n' "$paths" | grep -E '^(supabase|tests|loop|\.claude|\.github)/' || true)
  242: 
  243:           if [ -n "$lovable" ] && [ -n "$claude" ]; then
  244:             echo "::error::this pull request changes BOTH Lovable territory and Claude territory â€” split it into two pull requests"
  245:             echo "Lovable territory (src/):"
  246:             printf '%s\n' "$lovable" | sed 's/^/  /'
  247:             echo "Claude territory (supabase/, tests/, loop/, .claude/, .github/):"
  248:             printf '%s\n' "$claude" | sed 's/^/  /'
  249:             exit 1
  250:           fi
  251: 
  252:           if [ -n "$lovable" ]; then
  253:             echo "ownership guard OK â€” this pull request stays inside Lovable territory"
  254:           elif [ -n "$claude" ]; then
  255:             echo "ownership guard OK â€” this pull request stays inside Claude territory"
  256:           else
  257:             echo "ownership guard OK â€” this pull request touches neither territory"
  258:           fi
  259: 
  260:       - name: Guard against a pull request that names an item it does not own
  261:         if: github.event_name == 'pull_request'
  262:         shell: bash
  263:         env:
  264:           GH_TOKEN: ${{ github.token }}
  265:           REPOSITORY: ${{ github.repository }}
  266:           PR_NUMBER: ${{ github.event.pull_request.number }}
  267:           HEAD_REF: ${{ github.event.pull_request.head.ref }}
  268:         run: |
  269:           # THE ID ITSELF IS THE TRIGGER. Linear links a pull request to every item id in its
  270:           # title or body, and the link alone is enough to drag an item's status â€” a finished
  271:           # item was pulled back to In Progress twenty-four minutes after its own merge had
  272:           # correctly closed it, by a body that said `ref AI4DEV-43`. The deliberately
  273:           # "non-closing" words are NOT safe: ref, part of, related to, towards all do it.
  274:           # A CLOSING verb additionally closes the item, which is louder but not separate.
  275:           #
  276:           # This guard therefore fails on ANY id the branch does not own, not only on ids beside
  277:           # a closing verb. An earlier version checked only the verb and told authors to reach
  278:           # for `ref`/`towards` instead â€” advice that caused the exact defect it was preventing.
  279:           # Refer to other items IN WORDS: "the item that landed the relay".
  280:           #
  281:           # The hazard punishes good practice â€” the better the write-up, the more items it cites,
  282:           # and the most-cited item is the one somebody is actively working. Nothing checked that
  283:           # the pull request had any relationship to the item it closed, which is the same
  284:           # asserted-but-unobserved state this repository's way of work exists to delete.
  285:           #
  286:           # Title and body are read through the API rather than interpolated into this script.
  287:           # Expanding attacker-controlled text directly into a run block is script injection â€”
  288:           # and note that an expression cannot even be WRITTEN in one of these comments: an
  289:           # earlier draft spelled the syntax out here to explain the hazard, Actions evaluated
  290:           # it, and the workflow failed to parse. The warning broke the file by demonstrating
  291:           # itself.
  292:           if ! pr=$(gh api "repos/${REPOSITORY}/pulls/${PR_NUMBER}" --jq '.title + "\n" + (.body // "")'); then
  293:             echo "::error::the pull request's title and body could not be read from the GitHub API â€” the guard does not pass text it could not read"
  294:             exit 1
  295:           fi
  296: 
  297:           # No closing-verb regex any more. An earlier draft of this rewrite kept one to report
  298:           # whether a stray id would be CLOSED or merely reopened, but the proximity window
  299:           # reaches across the branch's own id, so "Closes AI4DEV-46, towards AI4DEV-22" reported
  300:           # AI4DEV-22 as a closure. Both outcomes fail identically, so the wrong-but-confident
  301:           # label bought nothing and was deleted rather than patched.
  302:           ID='AI4(DEV|PM)-[0-9]+'
  303: 
  304:           mentioned=$(printf '%s' "$pr" | grep -oiE "$ID" | tr '[:lower:]' '[:upper:]' | sort -u || true)
  305:           owned=$(printf '%s' "$HEAD_REF" | grep -oiE "$ID" | tr '[:lower:]' '[:upper:]' | sort -u || true)
  306: 
  307:           if [ -z "$mentioned" ]; then
  308:             echo "reference guard OK â€” the title and body name no item at all"
  309:             exit 0
  310:           fi
  311: 
  312:           echo "items named: $(printf '%s' "$mentioned" | tr '\n' ' ')"
  313:           echo "this branch owns: $(printf '%s' "${owned:-none}" | tr '\n' ' ')"
  314: 
  315:           stray=$(comm -23 <(printf '%s\n' "$mentioned" | grep . | sort -u) <(printf '%s\n' "$owned" | grep . | sort -u) || true)
  316:           if [ -n "$stray" ]; then
  317:             echo "::error::this pull request names an item its branch does not own â€” the link alone moves that item"
  318:             printf '%s\n' "$stray" | sed 's/^/  would move: /'
  319:             echo "The branch names $(printf '%s' "${owned:-no item}" | tr '\n' ' '), so any other id here moves an item by prose, not by the work."
  320:             echo "Fix the wording: name other items IN WORDS, never by id â€” 'the item that landed the relay'. There is no safe reference word; ref, part of, related to and towards all link and move the item."
  321:             exit 1
  322:           fi
  323: 
  324:           echo "reference guard OK â€” every item named belongs to this branch's own item"
FILE: loop/work/attribution-report.ps1
    1: # attribution-report.ps1 - the buildout's token-denominated burn report (attribution dogfood, AI4DEV-14).
    2: # Read-only over this machine's Claude Code transcripts for THIS project. Correlates each
    3: # response's PROVIDER-ECHOED token usage with the item that response was working on, and rolls
    4: # that up the attribution chain (REQ-034's model: token-denominated, honest buckets, totals that
    5: # reconcile).
    6: #   powershell -File loop/work/attribution-report.ps1                    -> all sessions
    7: #   powershell -File loop/work/attribution-report.ps1 -Days 1            -> touched in the last day
    8: #   powershell -File loop/work/attribution-report.ps1 -Item AI4DEV-79    -> one item only
    9: param([int]$Days = 0, [string]$Item = '')
   10: $ErrorActionPreference = 'Stop'
   11: 
   12: # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   13: # WHY ATTRIBUTION COMES FROM THE BRANCH, NOT FROM THE STAMP (founder, 2026-08-07).
   14: #
   15: # The stamp is a UserPromptSubmit hook, so it NEVER fires in a subagent thread - and the subagents
   16: # are where the work happens. On AI4DEV-48 the item ran as five orchestrator sittings, an executor,
   17: # distillers and a mechanical, and this report saw NINE responses for it, because it only read
   18: # main-session transcripts and only looked for a stamp.
   19: #
   20: # Every transcript record - main session and agent alike - carries `gitBranch`. The branch names
   21: # exactly one item, and it is trustworthy for the same reason the stamp trusts it: the branch is
   22: # COUPLED TO CLOSURE, since its pull request closes that item. A wrong branch surfaces as a wrong
   23: # closure on the board rather than as a quiet error in a ledger nobody reads.
   24: #
   25: # An earlier design recorded an agent-to-item map at spawn time. The founder killed it: the agents
   26: # form a TREE (the coordinator spawns a conductor, which spawns sittings, which spawn executors),
   27: # so a map captures one edge of many - and a declared fact that drifts is the failure class this
   28: # project exists to delete. The branch is on every record, at any nesting depth, declared by nobody.
   29: #
   30: # WHAT THIS UNDERCOUNTS, stated rather than hidden:
   31: #   - work done before an agent switches off `main` or its generated `worktree-agent-*` branch,
   32: #   - the reviewers entirely: codex and kimi are other vendors' processes and appear in no
   33: #     Claude transcript, so an item's true cost is higher than any figure here,
   34: #   - a branch naming two items, which resolves to unresolved rather than guessing.
   35: # So every number below is a FLOOR, not a total.
   36: # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   37: 
   38: $projDir = Join-Path $env:USERPROFILE '.claude\projects\C--Users-nirdr-Downloads-ai4good'
   39: if (-not (Test-Path $projDir)) { throw ('transcript directory not found: ' + $projDir) }
   40: $files = @(Get-ChildItem $projDir -Filter '*.jsonl' -File)
   41: 
   42: # Agent transcripts live under the session temp directories, NOT beside the main sessions - which
   43: # is why they were invisible to this report for its whole life.
   44: $agentRoot = Join-Path $env:LOCALAPPDATA 'Temp\claude\C--Users-nirdr-Downloads-ai4good'
   45: if (Test-Path $agentRoot) {
   46:     $files += @(Get-ChildItem $agentRoot -Filter '*.output' -File -Recurse -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue)
   47: }
   48: if ($Days -gt 0) { $files = @($files | Where-Object { $_.LastWriteTime -gt (Get-Date).AddDays(-$Days) }) }
   49: 
   50: # -Item normalisation: accept AI4DEV-79, ai4dev-79, or a bare 79, and turn it into the canonical id.
   51: $ItemFilter = ''
   52: if ($Item) {
   53:     $im = [regex]::Match($Item, '(?i)(?:AI4(DEV|PM)-)?0*([0-9]{1,7})')
   54:     if (-not $im.Success) { throw ('unrecognised -Item value: ' + $Item) }
   55:     $prefix = if ($im.Groups[1].Value) { $im.Groups[1].Value.ToUpperInvariant() } else { 'DEV' }
   56:     $ItemFilter = 'AI4' + $prefix + '-' + [string][int]$im.Groups[2].Value
   57: }
   58: 
   59: # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   60: # ROLE COMES FROM THE SPAWN CALL, NOT FROM A `ROLE:` LINE IN THE TRANSCRIPT (fixed 2026-08-10).
   61: # The premise the old detector rested on - "the contract's `ROLE:` line reaches the transcript" -
   62: # is FALSE for the current agents: a conductor's own `.output` file contains no `ROLE: conductor`.
   63: # The only `ROLE:` strings that appear are ones an agent READ from a sibling contract as a tool
   64: # result, so the detector reported the file it read (distiller) rather than the agent it was
   65: # (reviewer-runner) - a confident-wrong answer.
   66: # The honest signal: the PARENT records `subagent_type` when it spawns, and the spawn's tool_result
   67: # hands back the child's `agentId`. Each agent transcript is the file `<agentId>.output`. So a
   68: # first pass pairs the two by the shared tool_use id, building agentId -> role; the second pass
   69: # reads each file's role from its own name. A main-session `.jsonl` was spawned by nobody and is
   70: # the coordinator by definition.
   71: $roleOfAgent = @{}
   72: $typeRe   = [regex]'"subagent_type"\s*:\s*"([a-z][a-z0-9-]{2,30})"'
   73: $tuIdRe   = [regex]'"(?:id|tool_use_id)"\s*:\s*"(toolu_[A-Za-z0-9]+)"'
   74: $agentIdRe= [regex]'agentId:\s*(a[0-9a-f]{6,})'
   75: foreach ($f in $files) {
   76:     try {
   77:         $fs = New-Object System.IO.FileStream($f.FullName, [System.IO.FileMode]::Open, [System.IO.FileAccess]::Read, ([System.IO.FileShare]::ReadWrite -bor [System.IO.FileShare]::Delete))
   78:         $rd = New-Object System.IO.StreamReader($fs)
   79:     } catch { continue }
   80:     $pending = @{}   # tool_use id -> subagent_type, within THIS parent transcript
   81:     try {
   82:         while ($null -ne ($line = $rd.ReadLine())) {
   83:             if ($line.IndexOf('"subagent_type"') -ge 0) {
   84:                 $tm = $typeRe.Match($line); $im2 = $tuIdRe.Match($line)
   85:                 if ($tm.Success -and $im2.Success) { $pending[$im2.Groups[1].Value] = $tm.Groups[1].Value }
   86:             }
   87:             elseif ($line.IndexOf('agentId:') -ge 0) {
   88:                 $am = $agentIdRe.Match($line); $im2 = $tuIdRe.Match($line)
   89:                 if ($am.Success -and $im2.Success -and $pending.ContainsKey($im2.Groups[1].Value)) {
   90:                     $roleOfAgent[$am.Groups[1].Value] = $pending[$im2.Groups[1].Value]
   91:                 }
   92:             }
   93:         }
   94:     } finally { $rd.Dispose() }
   95: }
   96: 
   97: # The tag is still read as a FALLBACK for old sessions whose branches predate the id convention.
   98: # Quotes arrive escaped (\") because a transcript is JSONL; the original pattern was written
   99: # against the tag as it renders on screen and therefore matched nothing from the day it was
  100: # written. Matches containing braces are refused - a transcript that READ this hook's source
  101: # contains the tag's own format template, and it attributed real tokens to buckets named {0}.
  102: $stampNew = [regex]'<ai4good-attribution pm=\\?"([^"\\]*)\\?" dev=\\?"([^"\\]*)\\?"/>'
  103: $stampOld = [regex]'<ai4good-attribution wave=\\?"([^"\\]*)\\?" project=\\?"([^"\\]*)\\?" bucket=\\?"([^"\\]*)\\?"/>'
  104: $branchRe = [regex]'"gitBranch":"([^"]*)"'
  105: # Same tokeniser the stamp uses: strict boundaries, zero-padding normalised, ALL matches collected
  106: # so a branch naming two items is unresolved rather than "take the first".
  107: # CASE-INSENSITIVE, and that is not a detail: Linear's branch names are lower-case
  108: # (`nirdrang/ai4dev-48-...`), so a case-sensitive pattern matches NOTHING and every response
  109: # silently falls back to the stamp - which is the same shape of failure this file already carries
  110: # two scars from. Verified against a real branch before being believed.
  111: $idRe = New-Object System.Text.RegularExpressions.Regex(
  112:     '(?<![A-Za-z0-9])AI4(DEV|PM)-0*([0-9]{1,7})(?![A-Za-z0-9])',
  113:     ([System.Text.RegularExpressions.RegexOptions]::IgnoreCase -bor [System.Text.RegularExpressions.RegexOptions]::CultureInvariant))
  114: 
  115: function Get-BranchItems([string]$branch) {
  116:     $ids = @()
  117:     foreach ($m in $idRe.Matches($branch)) {
  118:         $id = 'AI4' + $m.Groups[1].Value.ToUpperInvariant() + '-' + [string][int]$m.Groups[2].Value
  119:         if ($ids -notcontains $id) { $ids += $id }
  120:     }
  121:     return $ids
  122: }
  123: 
  124: # ---- the chains, from the cache /work writes (never a Linear call from a report)
  125: $chains = @{}
  126: $attrDir = Join-Path $env:LOCALAPPDATA 'ai4good-build\nirdrang-ai4good\attr'
  127: foreach ($f in (Get-ChildItem $attrDir -Filter 'chain-*.json' -File -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue)) {
  128:     try {
  129:         $j = Get-Content $f.FullName -Raw | ConvertFrom-Json
  130:         $item = [string]$j.item
  131:         $nodes = @($j.chain | ForEach-Object { [string]$_.id })
  132:         # Duplicates exist (the same item resolved in two worktrees). Keep the LONGEST chain: a
  133:         # shorter one is a walk that stopped early, and claiming a nearer root than the real one
  134:         # is the confident-wrong answer this design forbids.
  135:         if ($item -and $nodes.Count -gt 0) {
  136:             if (-not $chains.ContainsKey($item) -or $nodes.Count -gt $chains[$item].Count) { $chains[$item] = $nodes }
  137:         }
  138:     } catch { }
  139: }
  140: 
  141: $agg = @{}   # key "item|source" -> counters
  142: $roleAgg = @{}  # key "item|role" -> counters
  143: $agentItem = @{}  # agent id (the task file's base name) -> the item it worked on
  144: function Add-Usage([string]$key, $usage, [hashtable]$agg) {
  145:     if (-not $agg.ContainsKey($key)) {
  146:         $agg[$key] = @{ responses = 0; inTok = [long]0; outTok = [long]0; cacheRead = [long]0; cacheWrite = [long]0 }
  147:     }
  148:     $a = $agg[$key]
  149:     $a.responses++
  150:     if ($usage.input_tokens)                { $a.inTok      += [long]$usage.input_tokens }
  151:     if ($usage.output_tokens)               { $a.outTok     += [long]$usage.output_tokens }
  152:     if ($usage.cache_read_input_tokens)     { $a.cacheRead  += [long]$usage.cache_read_input_tokens }
  153:     if ($usage.cache_creation_input_tokens) { $a.cacheWrite += [long]$usage.cache_creation_input_tokens }
  154: }
  155: 
  156: $sessions = 0
  157: $skipped = 0
  158: foreach ($f in $files) {
  159:     $curBranch = ''          # derived, primary
  160:     $curStamp = ''           # declared, fallback only
  161:     # role from the spawn map: an `.output` file is named by its agentId; a main `.jsonl` session
  162:     # was spawned by nobody and is the coordinator.
  163:     $base = [System.IO.Path]::GetFileNameWithoutExtension($f.Name)
  164:     $curRole = if ($f.Extension -eq '.output') { if ($roleOfAgent.ContainsKey($base)) { $roleOfAgent[$base] } else { 'unmarked agent' } } else { 'coordinator' }
  165:     # Shared read: a transcript belonging to a RUNNING agent or background task is locked, and a
  166:     # report that dies on a live file can never be run while anything is working - which is
  167:     # exactly when it is most worth running.
  168:     try {
  169:         $fs = New-Object System.IO.FileStream($f.FullName, [System.IO.FileMode]::Open, [System.IO.FileAccess]::Read, ([System.IO.FileShare]::ReadWrite -bor [System.IO.FileShare]::Delete))
  170:         $reader = New-Object System.IO.StreamReader($fs)
  171:     }
  172:     catch { $skipped++; continue }
  173:     $sessions++
  174:     try {
  175:         while ($null -ne ($line = $reader.ReadLine())) {
  176:             if ($line.IndexOf('"gitBranch"') -ge 0) {
  177:                 $bm = $branchRe.Match($line)
  178:                 if ($bm.Success) { $curBranch = $bm.Groups[1].Value }
  179:             }
  180:             if ($line.IndexOf('ai4good-attribution') -ge 0) {
  181:                 $m = $stampNew.Match($line)
  182:                 if ($m.Success -and ($m.Value -notmatch '[{}]')) {
  183:                     $d = $m.Groups[2].Value
  184:                     $curStamp = if ($d -and $d -ne 'none' -and $d -ne '-') { $d } else { '' }
  185:                 }
  186:                 else {
  187:                     $m = $stampOld.Match($line)
  188:                     if ($m.Success -and ($m.Value -notmatch '[{}]')) {
  189:                         $b = $m.Groups[3].Value
  190:                         $curStamp = if ($b) { 'legacy:' + $b } else { '' }
  191:                     }
  192:                 }
  193:             }
  194:             if ($line.IndexOf('"usage"') -ge 0 -and $line.IndexOf('"output_tokens"') -ge 0) {
  195:                 try {
  196:                     $o = $line | ConvertFrom-Json
  197:                     $u = $null
  198:                     if ($o.message -and $o.message.usage) { $u = $o.message.usage }
  199:                     elseif ($o.usage) { $u = $o.usage }
  200:                     if ($u -and $o.type -eq 'assistant') {
  201:                         $ids = @(Get-BranchItems $curBranch)
  202:                         if ($ids.Count -eq 1)      { Add-Usage ($ids[0] + '|branch')  $u $agg }
  203:                         elseif ($ids.Count -gt 1)  { Add-Usage ('unresolved|branch')  $u $agg }
  204:                         elseif ($curStamp)         { Add-Usage ($curStamp + '|stamp') $u $agg }
  205:                         else                       { Add-Usage ('unattributed|none')  $u $agg }
  206:                         if ($ids.Count -eq 1) {
  207:                             Add-Usage ($ids[0] + '|' + $curRole) $u $roleAgg
  208:                             $agentItem[$base] = $ids[0]
  209:                         }
  210:                     }
  211:                 } catch { }
  212:             }
  213:         }
  214:     }
  215:     finally { $reader.Dispose() }
  216: }
  217: 
  218: # ---- per item
  219: $rows = @()
  220: foreach ($k in $agg.Keys) {
  221:     $item, $src = $k -split '\|'
  222:     $a = $agg[$k]
  223:     $rows += [pscustomobject]@{ Item=$item; From=$src; Responses=$a.responses; OutputTok=$a.outTok; InputTok=$a.inTok; CacheRead=$a.cacheRead; CacheWrite=$a.cacheWrite }
  224: }
  225: $rows = $rows | Sort-Object -Property @{e='OutputTok';Descending=$true}
  226: 
  227: # ---- rolled up the chain: every ancestor carries its descendants' cost, and the item carries its own
  228: $roll = @{}
  229: function Add-Roll([string]$node, $r, [hashtable]$roll) {
  230:     if (-not $roll.ContainsKey($node)) { $roll[$node] = @{ responses = 0; outTok = [long]0; items = @() } }
  231:     $roll[$node].responses += $r.Responses
  232:     $roll[$node].outTok    += $r.OutputTok
  233:     if ($roll[$node].items -notcontains $r.Item) { $roll[$node].items += $r.Item }
  234: }
  235: foreach ($r in $rows) {
  236:     if ($r.Item -notmatch '^AI4(DEV|PM)-[0-9]+$') { continue }
  237:     if ($chains.ContainsKey($r.Item)) { foreach ($n in $chains[$r.Item]) { Add-Roll $n $r $roll } }
  238:     else { Add-Roll '(chain not cached)' $r $roll; Add-Roll $r.Item $r $roll }
  239: }
  240: $rollRows = @()
  241: foreach ($k in $roll.Keys) {
  242:     $rollRows += [pscustomobject]@{ Node=$k; Covers=$roll[$k].items.Count; Responses=$roll[$k].responses; OutputTok=$roll[$k].outTok }
  243: }
  244: $rollRows = $rollRows | Sort-Object -Property @{e='OutputTok';Descending=$true}
  245: 
  246: # ---- vendor spend (codex), joined by the session id the reviewer printed into its own log
  247: #
  248: # Every codex run writes a header containing `session id: <uuid>`, and we COMMIT those logs into
  249: # loop/items/<ITEM>/ - so the ids live in the repository and survive the artifact sweep. Codex
  250: # stores each run as sessions/<y>/<m>/<d>/rollout-<timestamp>-<uuid>.jsonl carrying real token
  251: # counts. Joining the two attributes reviewer spend to the item whose folder holds the log:
  252: # derived from what the tool emitted and what the item recorded, declared by nobody.
  253: #
  254: # KIMI IS INCLUDED, and an earlier version of this file claimed it could not be. That claim was
  255: # FALSE and was written after a search that used `-SimpleMatch` with a pattern containing `|`,
  256: # so it looked for the literal pipes instead of the alternatives and found nothing. The founder
  257: # pointed at the real location. Kimi keeps
  258: # ~/.kimi-code/sessions/wd_agent-<agentId>_*/session_*/agents/*/wire.jsonl, whose usage records
  259: # read {inputOther, output, inputCacheRead, inputCacheCreation} and are PER TURN - so they are
  260: # summed, unlike codex's cumulative total. The directory name embeds the id of the agent that
  261: # launched it, and that agent's own transcript gives the item, so the join needs nothing declared.
  262: $vendor = @{}
  263: $itemsDir = Join-Path (Split-Path -Parent (Split-Path -Parent $PSScriptRoot)) 'loop\items'
  264: $sessRe = [regex]'session id:\s*([0-9a-fA-F-]{36})'
  265: $rollouts = @{}
  266: $codexSessions = Join-Path $env:USERPROFILE '.codex\sessions'
  267: if (Test-Path $codexSessions) {
  268:     foreach ($rf in (Get-ChildItem $codexSessions -Recurse -File -Filter 'rollout-*.jsonl' -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue)) {
  269:         $mm = [regex]::Match($rf.Name, '([0-9a-fA-F-]{36})\.jsonl$')
  270:         if ($mm.Success) { $rollouts[$mm.Groups[1].Value.ToLower()] = $rf.FullName }
  271:     }
  272: }
  273: if (Test-Path $itemsDir) {
  274:     foreach ($dir in (Get-ChildItem $itemsDir -Directory -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue)) {
  275:         $item = $dir.Name
  276:         # -Recurse: since 2026-08-09 reviewer logs land in loop/items/<ITEM>/artifacts/, one level down
  277:         foreach ($log in (Get-ChildItem $dir.FullName -File -Filter '*stderr*' -Recurse -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue)) {
  278:             foreach ($sm in $sessRe.Matches([System.IO.File]::ReadAllText($log.FullName))) {
  279:                 $sid = $sm.Groups[1].Value.ToLower()
  280:                 if (-not $rollouts.ContainsKey($sid)) { continue }
  281:                 if (-not $vendor.ContainsKey($item)) { $vendor[$item] = @{ runs = 0; inTok = [long]0; outTok = [long]0; cached = [long]0 } }
  282:                 $v = $vendor[$item]; $v.runs++
  283:                 foreach ($l in [System.IO.File]::ReadLines($rollouts[$sid])) {
  284:                     if ($l.IndexOf('"output_tokens"') -lt 0) { continue }
  285:                     try {
  286:                         $o = $l | ConvertFrom-Json
  287:                         $tu = $null
  288:                         if ($o.payload -and $o.payload.info -and $o.payload.info.total_token_usage) { $tu = $o.payload.info.total_token_usage }
  289:                         elseif ($o.info -and $o.info.total_token_usage) { $tu = $o.info.total_token_usage }
  290:                         if ($tu) {
  291:                             # total_token_usage is CUMULATIVE for the session, so the last one wins
  292:                             # rather than being summed - adding them would multiply the real cost.
  293:                             $v.inTok = [long]$tu.input_tokens; $v.outTok = [long]$tu.output_tokens
  294:                             if ($tu.cached_input_tokens) { $v.cached = [long]$tu.cached_input_tokens }
  295:                         }
  296:                     } catch { }
  297:                 }
  298:             }
  299:         }
  300:     }
  301: }
  302: 
  303: $kimi = @{}
  304: $kimiRoot = Join-Path $env:USERPROFILE '.kimi-code\sessions'
  305: if (Test-Path $kimiRoot) {
  306:     foreach ($wd in (Get-ChildItem $kimiRoot -Directory -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue)) {
  307:         $am = [regex]::Match($wd.Name, 'wd_agent-([A-Za-z0-9]+)_')
  308:         if (-not $am.Success) { continue }
  309:         $agentId = $am.Groups[1].Value
  310:         if (-not $agentItem.ContainsKey($agentId)) { continue }
  311:         $item = $agentItem[$agentId]
  312:         foreach ($wire in (Get-ChildItem $wd.FullName -Recurse -File -Filter 'wire.jsonl' -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue)) {
  313:             if (-not $kimi.ContainsKey($item)) { $kimi[$item] = @{ sessions = 0; inTok = [long]0; outTok = [long]0; cached = [long]0 } }
  314:             $kimi[$item].sessions++
  315:             foreach ($l in [System.IO.File]::ReadLines($wire.FullName)) {
  316:                 if ($l.IndexOf('"usage"') -lt 0) { continue }
  317:                 try {
  318:                     $o = $l | ConvertFrom-Json
  319:                     $u2 = $o.usage
  320:                     if ($u2) {
  321:                         if ($u2.inputOther)         { $kimi[$item].inTok  += [long]$u2.inputOther }
